WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.810 --> 00:00:04.380
Did you even know Kate Winset was in "Avatar:
The Way of Water?"

00:00:04.380 --> 00:00:07.310
Did Quaritch and Spider's relationship feel
forced?

00:00:07.310 --> 00:00:10.617
Here's the dumbest things that happened in
the "Avatar" sequel!

00:00:11.294 --> 00:00:15.969
"Avatar: The Way of Water" is stuck in a strange
limbo: It's both a sequel, picking up where

00:00:15.969 --> 00:00:21.039
"Avatar" left off in a literal sense, but
also clearly made with a nostalgic zeal to

00:00:21.039 --> 00:00:23.039
remind people of the original.

00:00:23.039 --> 00:00:26.920
Think of the way the "Jurassic World" movies
feel more like "Jurassic Park" theme park

00:00:26.920 --> 00:00:30.510
rides than actual extensions of a coherent
story.

00:00:30.510 --> 00:00:34.360
The dumbest thing in "The Way of Water" that
we just have to live with is how it shoe-horns

00:00:34.360 --> 00:00:39.410
new roles for Sigourney Weaver and Stephen
Lang into the story, even though both of their

00:00:39.410 --> 00:00:41.989
characters died in the first movie.

00:00:41.989 --> 00:00:46.730
Weaver's new character Kiri, a child mysteriously
born of the Avatar of her original character

00:00:46.730 --> 00:00:51.850
Dr. Grace Augustine, at least has some strange
messianic qualities that are unique.

00:00:51.850 --> 00:00:56.760
But asking us to just roll with Lang's psychotic
Colonel Quaritch being reborn as a "Recombinant"

00:00:56.760 --> 00:00:58.620
form of Avatar is a bit much.

00:00:58.620 --> 00:01:03.420
It retcons "Avatar" in the sense that the
technology to create a digital copy of a human

00:01:03.420 --> 00:01:08.780
consciousness apparently existed at the time,
and Quaritch and his team backed themselves

00:01:08.780 --> 00:01:10.619
up before the final showdown.

00:01:10.619 --> 00:01:14.740
So essentially, like the ill-fated ending
of "Lost," "The Way of Water" is asking us

00:01:14.740 --> 00:01:17.560
to invest in a world where death has no meaning.

00:01:17.560 --> 00:01:21.100
Were Grace Augustine and the other scientists
also backed up?

00:01:21.100 --> 00:01:25.441
That would certainly explain why her Avatar
is just hanging around inert in a tank, for

00:01:25.441 --> 00:01:27.000
no discernible purpose.

00:01:27.701 --> 00:01:32.930
The biggest criticism of the first "Avatar"
is that Jake Sully is a classic white savior.

00:01:32.930 --> 00:01:37.570
It's tone deaf that way that a complete stranger
to the Na'vi way of life ends up uniting the

00:01:37.570 --> 00:01:40.640
tribes and being chosen as Turok Makto.

00:01:40.640 --> 00:01:45.280
You'd think years later in "The Way of Water,"
since Jake has integrated more fully and had

00:01:45.280 --> 00:01:49.619
half-Na'vi children with Neytiri, that this
would be less of a prevalent theme.

00:01:49.619 --> 00:01:54.309
But it seems Kiri has been added to the story
as a second white savior for the price of one.

00:01:54.309 --> 00:01:59.620
Though raised as a Na'vi, she's essentially
a sort of reincarnated version of Grace Augustine,

00:01:59.620 --> 00:02:03.170
the other most benevolent white person from
the first movie.

00:02:03.170 --> 00:02:07.920
Kiri clearly has some "chosen one" type powers
brewing as she communes with nature and can

00:02:07.920 --> 00:02:10.830
hear the heartbeat of Eywa, the all mother.

00:02:10.830 --> 00:02:14.970
Not to mention, "The Way of Water" goes the
same route as "Star Wars: The Phantom Menace"

00:02:14.970 --> 00:02:20.580
in introducing the idea of immaculate conception
with a couple of casual off-hand remarks.

00:02:20.580 --> 00:02:25.300
It's apparently "a total mystery" how Grace
Augustine's comatose Avatar gave birth to

00:02:25.300 --> 00:02:30.330
a child, although the movie pulls at a very
disturbing thread a couple of times as Kiri

00:02:30.330 --> 00:02:34.263
and other characters wonder who her father
might be.

00:02:34.263 --> 00:02:39.400
"Something really interesting going on there
biologically.

00:02:40.010 --> 00:02:42.129
I would die to get samples."

00:02:42.951 --> 00:02:48.580
Few sins of the original "Avatar" as unforgivable
as "unobtanium," the precious mineral that

00:02:48.580 --> 00:02:50.959
humans were on Pandora to harvest.

00:02:50.959 --> 00:02:54.680
Fortunately, no one utters that ridiculous
word in "The Way of Water."

00:02:54.680 --> 00:02:59.110
Although the reappearance of humanity is abruptly
handled, we do learn early on that they have

00:02:59.110 --> 00:03:03.660
a much more straightforward and urgent reason
to return to Pandora than getting rich.

00:03:03.660 --> 00:03:10.010
That is, earth is dying, and Pandora is to
be entirely colonized as humanity's new home.

00:03:10.010 --> 00:03:14.170
This creates a compelling global threat to
all of the Na'vi clans that's not even based

00:03:14.170 --> 00:03:15.500
on a bad pun.

00:03:15.500 --> 00:03:19.300
But fully two thirds of the way through "The
Way of Water," we learn there's an entirely

00:03:19.300 --> 00:03:22.830
brand new magical substance that humanity
is after on Pandora.

00:03:22.830 --> 00:03:28.577
It's a serum harvested from the brains of
giant sentient whale-like creatures called Tulkun.

00:03:28.577 --> 00:03:33.909
This enzyme, called Amrite, apparently stops
the aging process entirely in humans.

00:03:33.909 --> 00:03:39.450
So the hunting of Tulkuns becomes another
lazy, obvious allegory for the awful colonizers

00:03:39.450 --> 00:03:45.349
not to respect sacred Na'vi traditions, since
the Metkayina bond with the Tulkun and are

00:03:45.349 --> 00:03:46.879
heartbroken at their loss.

00:03:46.879 --> 00:03:50.989
The humans, of course, harvest just about
a two liter soda bottle's worth of Amrite

00:03:50.989 --> 00:03:54.644
from a Tulkun the size of a battleship and
leave the rest to rot.

00:03:55.459 --> 00:03:59.030
Were you even aware that the great Kate Winslet
is in this movie?

00:03:59.030 --> 00:04:03.989
She provides the voice of Ronal, the wife
of the Metkayina's leader, and mostly glares

00:04:03.989 --> 00:04:07.409
disapprovingly at Jake and family for endangering
her tribe.

00:04:07.409 --> 00:04:12.650
"The Way of Water" introduces many new characters,
but not a single one has the star power of

00:04:12.650 --> 00:04:17.060
Winslet, an Oscar winner and one of the greatest
actresses of her generation.

00:04:17.060 --> 00:04:21.390
So it's a bit dumb when you realize that like
the majority of characters in this overstuffed

00:04:21.390 --> 00:04:25.910
movie, she didn't have time for any sort of
character arc or sub-plot of her own.

00:04:25.910 --> 00:04:30.039
While it's likely she has a bigger role to
play in future installments, it doesn't make

00:04:30.039 --> 00:04:33.920
her bit part in "The Way of Water" any less
of a missed opportunity.

00:04:33.920 --> 00:04:38.640
Other than mourning her "soul sister" Tulkun's
death and being very justifiably angry at

00:04:38.640 --> 00:04:43.910
the Sullys for bringing destruction with them
out to the islands, Ronal barely registers.

00:04:43.910 --> 00:04:48.480
This is why Kate Winslet had to hold her breath
underwater for more than seven minutes?

00:04:48.480 --> 00:04:50.458
"Yes, that's true."

00:04:50.700 --> 00:04:55.430
Probably the most preposterous running thread
in "Avatar: The Way of Water" is the relationship

00:04:55.430 --> 00:04:59.460
between the Recombinant Colonel Quaritch and
his long lost son, Spider.

00:04:59.460 --> 00:05:04.039
The kid is the most prominent fully human
character left in the story, having been left

00:05:04.039 --> 00:05:07.400
on Pandora as a small child and raised with
the Na'vi.

00:05:07.400 --> 00:05:12.050
"The Way of Water" wants us to buy into big
plot points pivoting on the burgeoning emotional

00:05:12.050 --> 00:05:17.370
bond between this child of two worlds and
the lab-grown Avatar of his deceased father.

00:05:17.370 --> 00:05:22.190
The problem with this is that Quaritch, in
either human or Avatar form, is by far the

00:05:22.190 --> 00:05:24.850
most one-dimensional character in either movie.

00:05:24.850 --> 00:05:29.410
He's nothing but a murderous soldier, and
he outright tells Spider that since he's technically

00:05:29.410 --> 00:05:32.560
a form of clone, he's not actually his father
anyway.

00:05:32.560 --> 00:05:36.340
Sure, Quaritch and Spider may have had a few
bonding moments while the latter was being

00:05:36.340 --> 00:05:39.770
held hostage, but it really didn't go far
enough.

00:05:39.770 --> 00:05:44.360
Then seemingly out of nowhere, Quaritch is
suddenly concerned about Spider when Neytiri

00:05:44.360 --> 00:05:46.530
threatens his life in a late standoff.

00:05:46.530 --> 00:05:50.120
Spider then saves Quaritch's life, which doesn't
feel earned at all.

00:05:50.120 --> 00:05:54.600
It also doesn't even really matter because
presumably the humans could just keep growing

00:05:54.600 --> 00:05:58.835
new Quaritch Recombinants and re-downloading
his consciousness anyway.

00:05:59.256 --> 00:06:03.810
James Cameron has had an incredibly successful
career, so perhaps he's earned a little bit

00:06:03.810 --> 00:06:05.210
of self-referential leeway.

00:06:05.210 --> 00:06:10.190
But we have to bring into question the sequence
near the end of "The Way of Water" where star-crossed

00:06:10.190 --> 00:06:15.831
crushes Lo'ak and Reya attempt to survive
by clambering around the outside of an over-turned ship.

00:06:15.831 --> 00:06:20.101
These moments are pretty shamelessly close
to the beats from Cameron's "Titanic," to

00:06:20.101 --> 00:06:22.720
the point that it takes you out of the movie
a little bit.

00:06:22.720 --> 00:06:27.220
It's already a bit much to ask us to just
roll with their romance, which lazily recreates

00:06:27.220 --> 00:06:30.870
the same beats as Jake and Neytiri's story
in the first movie.

00:06:30.870 --> 00:06:35.550
Just like his father, Lo'ak is a stranger
to the Metkayina clan but learns their ways

00:06:35.550 --> 00:06:37.120
and charms the chief's daughter.

00:06:37.120 --> 00:06:42.270
He also befriends a dangerous and sacred animal
that they revere, and is told "I see you"

00:06:42.270 --> 00:06:44.370
by Reya in a direct echo.

00:06:44.370 --> 00:06:46.660
"Alien, that's all they see!"

00:06:46.660 --> 00:06:48.470
"I see you."

00:06:48.470 --> 00:06:53.069
During the sequence when a whaling vessel
slowly turns upside down, Lo'ak and Reya end

00:06:53.069 --> 00:06:57.539
up precariously perched right on top of it
as it submerges in the water.

00:06:57.539 --> 00:07:00.650
Just like Jack and Rose in "Titanic," they
promise to hold on to each other when they

00:07:00.650 --> 00:07:01.650
take the plunge.

00:07:01.650 --> 00:07:03.350
What's James Cameron up to?

00:07:04.110 --> 00:07:08.240
One of the strangest scenes in "Avatar: The
Way of Water" is when Norm and Max fly out

00:07:08.240 --> 00:07:12.590
to the islands to try and see what's going
on with Kiri, who had a seizure while connected

00:07:12.590 --> 00:07:15.430
to the Metkayina version of the Spirit Tree.

00:07:15.430 --> 00:07:19.600
Attaching diodes and machines to
Kiri's head, Norm speculates that her spiritual

00:07:19.600 --> 00:07:22.830
connection to Eywa may be a side effect of
epilepsy.

00:07:22.830 --> 00:07:26.790
It's an abrupt and strange suggestion after
lots of evidence that there may be nothing

00:07:26.790 --> 00:07:28.849
special about Kiri at all.

00:07:28.849 --> 00:07:33.910
This flies in the face of the generally faith-forward
nature of both "Avatar" movies and never gets

00:07:33.910 --> 00:07:34.910
followed up on.

00:07:34.910 --> 00:07:40.965
In general, the spiritual purity of the Na'vi
and the real-world influence of Eywa is empirically obvious.

00:07:40.965 --> 00:07:45.600
In the first movie, the Na'vi basically are
losing the fight until Eywa intervenes and

00:07:45.600 --> 00:07:49.449
possesses all the animal life on the planet
to attack the human forces.

00:07:49.449 --> 00:07:54.639
"Avatar" as a franchise seems to have no interest
in sincerely exploring the delicate balance

00:07:54.639 --> 00:07:56.810
between science and faith.

00:07:56.810 --> 00:08:00.819
This scene, which dabbles in skepticism just
for the sake of a distraction, is one of the

00:08:00.819 --> 00:08:02.763
dumbest things in "The Way of Water."

00:08:03.385 --> 00:08:08.110
Most people don't go to the movies to read,
so subtitles can often be a deal breaker when

00:08:08.110 --> 00:08:10.139
you're browsing the Netflix menu.

00:08:10.139 --> 00:08:14.889
As Jake Sully learned the ways of the Omaticaya
Clan of the Na'vi in the first "Avatar," the

00:08:14.889 --> 00:08:20.210
movie was committed to showing both English
and Na'vi being spoken consistently, and subtitling

00:08:20.210 --> 00:08:22.389
the alien dialogue whenever necessary.

00:08:22.389 --> 00:08:25.880
The way language was treated was logical and
consistent.

00:08:25.880 --> 00:08:29.889
But in "The Way of Water," director James
Cameron and company must have realized early

00:08:29.889 --> 00:08:33.669
on that since Jake now has been living with
the Na'vi for more than a decade, they would

00:08:33.669 --> 00:08:36.260
essentially be making a foreign language film.

00:08:36.260 --> 00:08:40.440
To solve this, very early in the sequel, Jakes
tells us via voice-over that he's learned

00:08:40.440 --> 00:08:44.860
the Na'vi language so thoroughly that when
he hears it, it may as well be English.

00:08:44.860 --> 00:08:49.630
And presto, suddenly we also hear his children
and all other Na'vi speaking English.

00:08:49.630 --> 00:08:54.690
When Na'vi interact with other English-speaking
characters later, they're still subtitled,

00:08:54.690 --> 00:08:59.250
but whenever Na'vi are talking to each other,
it's all English for convenience's sake.

00:08:59.250 --> 00:09:04.339
It does save the audience a lot of subtitle
reading, but it's a bracingly dumb and inelegant

00:09:04.339 --> 00:09:08.579
tweak to the rules of the way the reality
of what we're seeing it being depicted.

00:09:08.579 --> 00:09:12.850
After the earnest pride that the first "Avatar"
took in realizing Na'vi culture, it's kind

00:09:12.850 --> 00:09:17.890
of surprising that "The Way of Water" just
shrugs and transforms 90% of potentially Na'vi

00:09:17.890 --> 00:09:19.963
dialogue into plain old English.

